{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "07-distributive.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Distributive and Boolean lattices",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Decide whether meet distributes over join, with the witness when it does not, and see why distributivity is what makes complements (\"not $x$\") unique.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "For sets, $\\cap$ distributes over $\\cup$: $A \\cap (B \\cup C) = (A \\cap B) \\cup (A \\cap C)$, the way $\\times$ distributes over $+$. Every lattice has a meet and a join. Does the meet always distribute over the join?\n\n> [!try]\n> M₃ below has a bottom, three incomparable atoms $a, b, c$, and a top. Compute both sides of $a \\wedge (b \\vee c) = (a \\wedge b) \\vee (a \\wedge c)$ by hand before running the check.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let M3 = poset({bot, a, b, c, top}; bot < a, bot < b, bot < c, a < top, b < top, c < top)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "distributive(M3)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The left side is $a \\wedge \\top = a$, the right $\\bot \\vee \\bot = \\bot$. The pentagon N₅ fails too:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let N5 = poset({bot, p, q, r, top}; bot < p, bot < q, q < r, p < top, r < top)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "distributive(N5)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Distributive lattice\n> A lattice is **distributive** when $x \\wedge (y \\vee z) = (x \\wedge y) \\vee (x \\wedge z)$ for all $x, y, z$ (the dual law then follows).",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Birkhoff\n> A lattice is distributive exactly when it contains neither M₃ nor N₅ as a sublattice. They are the witnesses every failure contains.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let D = divisors(12)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "distributive(D)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Complements",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Why care? Distributivity is what makes \"not $x$\" mean one thing.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Complement\n> In a lattice with $\\bot$ and $\\top$, a **complement** of $x$ is a $y$ with $x \\vee y = \\top$ and $x \\wedge y = \\bot$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "complement(M3, a)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In M₃, $a$ has two complements, so \"not $a$\" is ambiguous. In a distributive lattice that cannot happen. If $y$ and $y'$ are both complements of $x$, then\n$$y = y \\wedge (x \\vee y') = (y \\wedge x) \\vee (y \\wedge y') = \\bot \\vee (y \\wedge y') = y \\wedge y',$$\nand in the same way $y' = y \\wedge y'$, so $y = y'$. Distributivity was used once, in the middle step: it is exactly what the uniqueness needs. Among the divisors of 12, complements are unique, but some elements have none:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "complement(D, 4)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "complement(D, 2)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$2$ has no complement: $\\operatorname{lcm}(2, y) = 12$ forces $4 \\mid y$ and $3 \\mid y$, so $y = 12$, and then $\\gcd(2, 12) = 2$, not $1$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Boolean lattices",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Boolean lattice\n> A **Boolean lattice** is distributive and complemented: every element has a complement, and only one. The subsets of a set are the model: complement is set complement.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let P = subsets({x, y, z})",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "boolean(P)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let D30 = divisors(30)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "boolean(D30)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "complement(D30, 6)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The divisors of 30 are the subsets of $\\{2, 3, 5\\}$ in disguise: a squarefree number is the set of its primes.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem bool_distrib (a b c : Bool) : (a && (b || c)) = ((a && b) || (a && c)) := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that the Booleans are distributive.",
      "leanSolution": "  cases a <;> cases b <;> cases c <;> rfl",
      "hints": [
        "There are eight cases: `cases a <;> cases b <;> cases c`, and each is `rfl`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "complement(M3, b)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "List the complements of $b$ in M₃.",
      "hints": [
        "Which elements join with $b$ to $\\top$ and meet it at $\\bot$?"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "boolean(D)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Are the divisors of 12 a Boolean lattice?",
      "hints": [
        "Does every element have a complement?"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "complement(D30, 10)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "List the complements of 10 among the divisors of 30.",
      "hints": [
        "$10 = 2 \\cdot 5$: which primes are missing?"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "complement(N5, p)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Predict first: are complements unique in the pentagon N₅? Then list the complements of $p$.",
      "hints": [
        "Try each element $y$: is $p \\vee y = \\top$ and $p \\wedge y = \\bot$?",
        "N₅ is not distributive, so nothing forces a single answer."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Distributivity can fail, and M₃ and N₅ are why. It is what makes complements unique. Boolean lattices are distributive with complements; the subsets of a set, and the divisors of a squarefree number, are the examples.\n\nNext, a small lattice with a job: the answers an access rule can give, and the ways a policy combines them.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
